@Tutorial(time: 30) {
    @Intro(title: "Spawning monsters") {
        In this part, we're going to spawn monsters along a path randomly. By the
        end, you will have monsters roaming the game board.

        @Image(source: 05-01.monsters-path-preview.png)
    }
    @Section(title: "Modifying the project settings") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Before drawing the path, we're going to change the game resolution.
            Our game has a default window size of `1152x648`. We're going to set
            it to `720x540`, a nice little box.

            @Image(source: 05-02.window-size-settings.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Go back to the 3D view and open the `main` scene.

                @Image(source: 05-03.open-main-scene.png, alt: "The main.tscn scene open in the File Pad")
            }
            @Step {
                Tap the **Dropdown** toolbar button in the upper-right corner and choose
                **Settings** to open Project Settings.

                @Image(source: 05-04.project-settings-menu.png)
            }
            @Step {
                If you still have _Input Map_ open, switch to the _General_
                tab. In the left menu, navigate down to _Display -> Window_.
                On the right, set the _Viewport Width_ to `720` and the
                _Viewport Height_ to `540`.

                @Image(source: 05-05.window-size-720x540.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Scroll down to `stretch` and set the **Mode** to `viewport`.
                @Image(source: 05-05a.window-mode-viewport.png)
            }
            Now the game window will scale correctly when you view it on different screens.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Creating the spawn path") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Like you did in the 2D game tutorial, you're going to design a path
            and use a
            [PathFollow3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fpathfollow3d.html#class-pathfollow3d)
            node to sample random locations on it.

            In 3D though, it's a bit more complicated to draw the path. We
            want it to be around the game view so monsters appear right
            outside the screen. But if we draw a path, we won't see it from
            the camera preview.

            To find the view's limits, we can use some placeholder meshes. In
            3D, materials define a surface's visual properties like its color,
            how it reflects light, and more. We can use them to change the
            color of a mesh.

            @Image(source: 05-06.spawn-path-concept.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Your viewport should still be split into two parts, with the
                camera preview at the bottom. If that isn't the case, tap the
                **Cog** menu in the 3D editor toolbar and choose _Stacked_.
                Select the
                [Camera3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcamera3d.html#class-camera3d)
                node and tap the _Preview_ checkbox in the bottom viewport.

                @Image(source: 05-07.camera-preview-split.png)
            }
            Let's add the placeholder meshes.

            @Step {
                Add a new
                [Node3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fnode3d.html#class-node3d)
                as a child of the `Main` node and name it `Cylinders`. We'll
                use it to group the cylinders.

                @Image(source: 05-08.cylinders-node-added.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Select `Cylinders` and add a child node
                [MeshInstance3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fmeshinstance3d.html#class-meshinstance3d).
                In the _Inspector_, assign a _CylinderMesh_ to the _Mesh_
                property.

                @Image(source: 05-09.cylinder-mesh-instance.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Next, Open the _Surface Material Override_ menu and create a new _StandardMaterial3D_.
                
                Then, tap the new material to open it and change the _Albedo_ color to _Orange_ like so:
                
                @Image(source: 05-09a.cylinder-material-override-color.png)
            }
            Doing this step will help make the cylinders visible when we move them into position later.
            @Step {
                Set the top viewport to the top orthogonal view using the menu
                in the viewport's top-left corner. Tap _Perspective_ and then tap _Top_ and change _Perspective_ to _Orthagonal_. It should now say _TopOrthagonal_ in the bar.

                The grid may be distracting. You can toggle it by going to the
                _Cog_ menu in the toolbar and tapping the toggle next to _Show Grid_.

                @Image(source: 05-10.top-orthogonal-view.png)
            }
            You now want to move the cylinder along the ground plane, looking
            at the camera preview in the bottom viewport. I recommend using
            grid snap to do so.

            @Step {
                Toggle grid snap by tapping the `#` (Grid) icon and toggling _Snap_.

                @Image(source: 05-11.toggle-grid-snap.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Move the cylinder so it's right outside the camera's view in
                the top-left corner.

                @Image(source: 05-12.first-cylinder-positioned.png)
            }
            We're going to create copies of the mesh and place them around the
            game area.

            @Step {
                Long-press the node in the _Scene Tree_ and select
                _Duplicate_. Move the copy down along the blue Z axis until
                it's right outside the camera's preview.

                @Image(source: 05-13.second-cylinder-placed.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Select both cylinders by swiping right on each in the _Node Tree_, then duplicate them.

                @Image(source: 05-14.both-cylinders-selected.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Select the two new Cylinder meshes.
                
                Move them to the right by dragging the red X axis.

                @Image(source: 05-15.four-cylinders-corners.png)
            }

           We can now use the meshes as a guide.

            @Step {
                Fold them in the _Scene Tree_ by tapping the grey arrow next to
                the _Cylinders_ node. Moving forward, you can also toggle their visibility by
                tapping the eye icon next to _Cylinders_.

                @Image(source: 05-19.cylinders-folded-tree.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Select `Main` again and add a child node
                [Path3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fpath3d.html#class-path3d). 
                With the `Path3D` selected, in the main viewport, you'll notice a new bar with six icons 
                has appeared. Tap the _Add
                Point_ tool, the icon with the "+" sign in a circle.


                @Image(source: 05-20.path-add-point-tool.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Tap in the center of each cylinder to create a point. 
                Move in a clockwise direction from Top-Left to the right, 
                making your way to Bottom-Left. Then,
                tap the `...` menu and tap _Close Curve_ to close the path.
                If any point is a bit off, you can tap the Pointer icon on 
                the left of the menu and then select and drag on it to
                reposition it.

                Your path should look like this.

                @Image(source: 05-21.path-closed-complete.png)
            }
            To sample random positions on it, we need a
            [PathFollow3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fpathfollow3d.html#class-pathfollow3d)
            node.

            @Step {
                Add a `PathFollow3D` as a child of the `Path3D`. Rename the
                two nodes to `SpawnLocation` and `SpawnPath`, respectively.
                It's more descriptive of what we'll use them for.

                @Image(source: 05-22.spawn-path-nodes.png)
            }
            With that, we're ready to code the spawn mechanism.
            
            Save your work and move to the next section.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Spawning monsters randomly") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            In this section, we'll attach a script to the `Main` node and wire up a timer that
            spawns a new mob on each tick.

            @Image(source: 05-23.mob-spawning-logic.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Long-press the `Main` node and attach a new _Empty_ script to it, 
                saving it in the `scripts` folder.

                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-3d-00.gd, reset: true)
            }
            @Step {
                We first export a variable to the _Inspector_ so that we can
                assign `mob.tscn` or any other monster to it.

                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-3d-01.gd, previousFile: main-3d-00.gd)
            }
            @Step {
                Now select the `Main` node. Drag `mob.tscn` from the _File
                Pad_ to the _Mob Scene_ slot in the _Inspector_.

                @Image(source: 05-24.mob-scene-property-assigned.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Add a new
                [Timer](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Ftimer.html#class-timer)
                node as a child of `Main`. Name it `MobTimer`.

                @Image(source: 05-25.mob-timer-added.png)
            }
            @Step {
                In the _Inspector_, set its _Wait Time_ to `0.5` seconds and
                turn on _Autostart_ so it automatically starts when we run
                the game.

                @Image(source: 05-26.mob-timer-properties.png)
            }
            Timers emit a `timeout` signal every time they reach the end of
            their _Wait Time_. By default, they restart automatically,
            emitting the signal in a cycle. We can connect to this signal
            from the _Main_ node to spawn monsters every `0.5` seconds.

            @Step {
                With the _MobTimer_ still selected, head to the _Signals_ tab
                in the _Inspector_, and tap the `timeout` signal.

                @Image(source: 05-27.timeout-signal-selected.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Connect it to the _Main_ node.

                @Image(source: 05-28.connect-timer-to-main.png)
            }
            This will take you back to the script, with a new empty
            `_on_mob_timer_timeout()` function.

            Let's code the mob spawning logic. We're going to:

            1. Instantiate the mob scene.
            2. Sample a random position on the spawn path.
            3. Get the player's ground position (flattening the Y coordinate).
            4. Call the mob's `initialize()` method, passing it the random
               position and the player's position.
            5. Add the mob as a child of the _Main_ node.

            @Step {
                Add the spawn logic to the `_on_mob_timer_timeout` function.

                @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-3d-02.gd, previousFile: main-3d-01.gd)
            }
            Above, `randf()` produces a random value between `0` and `1`,
            which is what the _PathFollow_ node's `progress_ratio` expects:
            0 is the start of the path, 1 is the end of the path. The path
            we have set is around the camera's viewport, so any random
            value between 0 and 1 is a random position alongside the edges
            of the viewport!

            Notice we flatten the player's position to the ground plane by
            setting the Y component to `0`. This ensures mobs always face
            horizontally toward the player's ground position, even when the
            player is jumping. Without this, mobs would tilt up when the
            player is in the air, which looks strange.

            > Important: If you remove the `Player` from the main scene, the
            > line
            >
            > `var player_position = Vector3($Player.position.x, 0, $Player.position.z)`
            >
            > gives a "node not found" error because there is no $Player!

            @Step {
                You can test the scene by tapping the **Run Project** button in the toolbar.
                You should see the monsters spawn and move in a straight
                line.

                > Tip: If you can't see the new monsters, double-check that
                > the `MobTimer`'s `Autostart` is on and that the `timeout`
                > signal is connected to the `Main` node. The Inspector's
                > "Remote" tab also shows the running scene tree so you can
                > confirm the mob is being added.

                @Image(source: 05-29.mobs-spawning-in-game.png)
            }
            For now, they just slide across the scene. We'll refine how they interact with the player and
            each other when we set up physics layers and hit boxes in a
            later lesson. Next, let's add the ability to jump.
        }
    }
}
